WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.280
American Psycho might have seemed like a straightforward
film about a psychotic serial killer — one

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:08.090
who has good hygiene and knows his font types.

00:00:08.090 --> 00:00:12.510
But the third act of the movie intentionally
unravels and leaves us guessing about what,

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:16.910
if anything, actually happened to our deranged
protagonist throughout the whole film.

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:21.329
See, that's the trouble with having a delusional
maniac at the center of the plot — his memory

00:00:21.329 --> 00:00:25.529
of his own supposed murder spree might not
be so reliable after all.

00:00:25.529 --> 00:00:29.150
Like any good film, however, there are an
abundance of clues scattered throughout the

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:34.320
movie, and subsequent commentaries might help
fans parse out Patrick Bateman's reality from

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:35.460
his mental fiction.

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:39.850
So, let's take a look at what really happened
at the end of American Psycho.

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:41.110
The body count

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:45.290
There's one surefire fact that we can all
agree upon when it comes to Patrick Bateman:

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:46.670
the guy is crazy.

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:50.940
Whether he actually kills all the people he
thinks he does is up for debate, but his status

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:52.420
as a lunatic is not.

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:57.100
From his obsessive-compulsive fitness and
grooming regimens to his fixation with cardstock

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:59.930
and music history, he's clearly disturbed.

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:04.220
But has he actually killed all of those people,
or is he just imagining it all as some sort

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:05.560
of sick fetish fantasy?

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:08.759
"Part of me wants to take her out, and talk
to her.

00:01:08.759 --> 00:01:11.109
Be real nice and sweet and treat her right."

00:01:11.109 --> 00:01:13.030
"What'd the other part of him think?"

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:15.549
"What her head would look like on a stick."

00:01:15.549 --> 00:01:19.439
Some believe he's just dreaming up all those
intense slaughter sessions, and there's some

00:01:19.439 --> 00:01:22.149
serious evidence to back that idea up.

00:01:22.149 --> 00:01:26.289
Consider the time his ATM tells him to feed
it a stray cat before he shoots a walk-along

00:01:26.289 --> 00:01:27.469
witness for interfering.

00:01:27.469 --> 00:01:30.509
At least some part of that experience is a
hallucination.

00:01:30.509 --> 00:01:34.569
And the fact that he then engages in a shoot-out
with police that culminates in him blowing

00:01:34.569 --> 00:01:39.429
up their cruiser with just his glock was so
outlandish even he doesn't believe it's real.

00:01:39.429 --> 00:01:42.939
And isn't it a little suspicious that the
guy who can rattle off every shade of ink

00:01:42.939 --> 00:01:48.149
known to man and recite an entire encyclopedia
on Phil Collins somehow can't recall his own

00:01:48.149 --> 00:01:49.149
kill count?

00:01:49.149 --> 00:01:52.100
"I guess I've killed maybe 20 people.

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:53.299
Maybe 40!"

00:01:53.299 --> 00:01:57.420
Let's just say that Bateman takes the concept
of the "unreliable narrator" to a whole new

00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:58.420
level.

00:01:58.420 --> 00:01:59.420
However...

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:01.009
The convenient alibi

00:02:01.009 --> 00:02:05.530
The biggest mystery in the film revolves around
whether or not Patrick Bateman actually ever

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:08.860
killed his co-worker Paul Allen, played by
Jared Leto.

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:12.530
Paul's death scene would prove to be one of
the most memorable moments of the movie and

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:16.060
forever change how viewers experienced Huey
Lewis and the News' music.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:20.040
"I think their undisputed masterpiece is 'Hip
to Be Square.'

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:24.410
Most people probably don't listen to the lyrics,
but they should!"

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:28.920
Patrick is subsequently given an extremely
convenient alibi by the detective investigating

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:32.350
Paul's disappearance that may or may not be
the real deal.

00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:36.190
Before the murder, Patrick is shown going
to dinner with Paul under the name of Marcus

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:40.500
Halberstrom, another co-worker Paul has long
mistaken Patrick for.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:44.870
But when questioned, the real Marcus reveals
he was out at another location the night of

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:49.030
the murder… with Patrick Bateman himself
listed as part of that group.

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:52.890
So, either Patrick has actually gone to the
dinner with Marcus and his other work pals

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:56.510
while dreaming up his deadly encounter with
Paul — or he's done exactly what he thinks

00:02:56.510 --> 00:03:00.160
he has, and his colleagues mistakenly gift
him an alibi.

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:03.990
Considering how often all those Pierce &amp; Pierce
Vice Presidents seem to call each other by

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:08.850
the wrong names, the narrative definitely
seems to be leaning towards the latter.

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:10.210
The cover up?

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:14.150
One of the main reasons we're left to wonder
if any of these murders are real is that every

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:17.030
shred of evidence that might convict him disappears
overnight.

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:21.530
At the tail end of his murderous rampage,
Patrick has turned Paul's swanky apartment

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:24.230
into a mini-graveyard for his victims' bodies.

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:27.850
But when he returns to the scene of the crime,
the place is completely clean.

00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:32.030
It appears that the property owners have removed
all traces of wrongdoing and painted over

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:35.570
the blood-filled closet to ensure the ritzy
locale's high rental value.

00:03:35.570 --> 00:03:39.430
And the listing agent's attitude towards him
certainly betrays some suspicion …

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:41.820
"I want to know what happened here."

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:45.010
"Don't make any trouble, please.

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:47.820
I suggest you go."

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:51.990
Then there's the matter of his lawyer, whom
he'd feverishly confessed to via voicemail.

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:57.120
At first, Harold Carnes literally laughs off
his message, calls him by the name Davis,

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:01.120
and says Patrick Bateman is a sorry subject
choice for such a joke — because he's too

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:03.340
dorky to actually commit murder.

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:05.900
"That was you wasn't it.

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:09.010
Bateman killing Allen and the escort girls
that's fabulous, that's rich."

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:13.440
But then, when Patrick makes it clear he's
not joking, his attorney claims that there's

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:15.190
no way he could've killed Paul Allen …

00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:19.990
"Because I had dinner with Paul Allen twice
in London just ten days ago."

00:04:19.990 --> 00:04:22.049
"No you...."

00:04:22.049 --> 00:04:25.830
Could this man, who has called his own client
by the wrong name twice, have simply mistaken

00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:27.900
Paul Allen with someone else, too?

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:28.900
Sure.

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:32.050
Which is why it takes some explanation from
on high to really understand what was meant

00:04:32.050 --> 00:04:33.990
by these ambiguous scenes.

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:36.290
Let's hear from the folks who made this thing...

00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:38.100
The voice of authority

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:42.560
Director Mary Harron has since revealed that
she never intended for audiences to believe

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:43.680
Patrick was innocent.

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:48.670
"Everyone keeps coming out of the film thinking
that it's all a dream, and I never intended

00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:49.670
it."

00:04:49.670 --> 00:04:53.100
She went on to explain that the last scene
wasn't meant to make it seem like Patrick

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:57.580
Bateman can't separate his imagination from
reality, but rather that Carnes and the rest

00:04:57.580 --> 00:04:59.540
are the ones who are really confused.

00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:01.389
Not everything was real, of course.

00:05:01.389 --> 00:05:05.850
Screenwriter Guinevere Turner has said that
the turning point in Patrick's psychosis takes

00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:10.550
place when he gets absorbed in his own reflection
while sleeping with two prostitutes.

00:05:10.550 --> 00:05:15.130
She told Yahoo Movies, "in real life, they
probably weren't as attractive as they are,

00:05:15.130 --> 00:05:17.720
and it wasn't all as Penthouse Letters as
it is."

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:22.360
In other words, Patrick Bateman might've embellished
a few of the more outrageous details — but

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:24.389
he really was hurting people all the while.

00:05:24.389 --> 00:05:27.740
What really matters, however, is how others
around him react.

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:32.260
According to author Bret Easton Ellis, who
penned the novel upon which the film was based,

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:37.400
American Psycho is meant to be a statement
on male hedonism in the '80s Wall Street circuit.

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:41.450
The whole reason Bateman gets away with the
murder of Paul Allen is that everyone in Patrick's

00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:46.170
professional circle is so self-absorbed that
they can't even keep each other's identities

00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:47.170
straight.

00:05:47.170 --> 00:05:50.860
Their attention is firmly focused on acquiring
material wealth, and their biggest problems

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:55.020
revolve around getting dinner reservations
at the fanciest restaurant in town.

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:58.830
Even the owners of Allen's apartment are willing
to dispose of a serial killer's evidence to

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:00.730
ensure maximum profit.

00:06:00.730 --> 00:06:05.180
In the end, there are no redeeming qualities
about Bateman or anyone he knows, for that

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:06.180
matter.

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:09.010
And even when he tries to come clean about
what he's done, the people around him are

00:06:09.010 --> 00:06:11.280
too wrapped up in their own interests to care.

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:14.820
"Even after admitting this, there is no catharsis.

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:20.800
My punishment continues to elude me, and I
gain no deeper knowledge of myself."

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:22.110
Thanks for watching!

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:24.870
